      "difficulty": "easy",
      "text": "Find the least positive integer n such that 79 divides 7^n + 1. If no such n exists, answer NONE.",
      "exact_answer": "39",
      "strategy_applies": true,
      "features": {
        "modulus_structure": "prime",
        "base_coprime": "yes",
        "global_order_parity": "even",
        "local_order_2adic_pattern": "aligned_positive",
        "number_of_components": "1",
        "modulus_size": "small"
      },
      "human_solution": "Since gcd(7, 79) = 1, the base is a unit modulo 79. The multiplicative order of 7 modulo 79 is d = 78. If 7^n \u2261 -1 (mod 79) then squaring gives 7^(2n) \u2261 1, so d | 2n, while d does not divide n. Therefore n must be an odd multiple of d/2 = 39. Direct computation gives 7^39 \u2261 78 \u2261 -1 (mod 79), so the exponent d/2 does produce -1. The least such n is therefore 39.",
